    {
      "parameters": {
        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "=You are qualifying a local business as a sales lead for an agency that sells website + AI/automation upgrades. The IDEAL lead is a business that is ALREADY HEALTHY and ALREADY SPENDING money to get customers, but doing it with a fixable inefficiency. We are NOT looking for struggling businesses — a business with no money cannot buy. Money already moving is the signal: we offer a better deal than what they already pay for.\n\nBusiness data (a value of '—' means unknown/missing, NOT a real value):\n- Name: {{ $json.name }}\n- Website: {{ $json.website }}\n- Category: {{ $json.category }}\n- Rating: {{ $json.rating }}\n- Reviews: {{ $json.reviews }}\n- Running a Google Maps ad right now (isAdvertisement): {{ $json.isAdvertisement }}\n- Photos on their listing (imagesCount): {{ $json.imagesCount }}\n- Google listing claimed by owner: {{ $json.listingClaimed }}\n- Offers online estimates/booking: {{ $json.onlineEstimates }}\n- Email: {{ $json.email }}\n- Socials: {{ $json.socials }}\n\nCRITICAL — how to read the isAdvertisement field: a value of 'yes' means our scrape SAW them occupying a paid Google Maps ad slot. A value of 'no' or '—' means our scrape simply did NOT see them in an ad slot — that is UNKNOWN, and is NOT evidence that they are not advertising. Never treat 'no'/'—' as a gap or a deficiency, never subtract points for it, and NEVER mention ads in your reason unless the value is exactly 'yes'.\n\nScore from 1 to 10. Start at 3, then adjust:\n\nALREADY SPENDING / HEALTHY (they have budget — raise the score):\n- +3 if isAdvertisement is 'yes' (they are paying Google for a Maps ad slot right now — the strongest 'already buying leads' signal).\n- +2 if imagesCount is a real number and 25 or more (heavy investment in their presence — an established operation, not a one-person shop); +1 if imagesCount is between 10 and 24.\n- +2 if they have a real custom branded domain website (their own domain — NOT a Facebook/Instagram page, NOT wixsite/weebly/business.site, NOT '—').\n- +1 if rating is 4.0 or higher AND reviews is 40 or more (healthy, trusted, active).\n- +1 if they have active social profiles listed.\n\nFIXABLE INEFFICIENCY (the thing we would sell — raise the score):\n- +2 if 'Offers online estimates/booking' is 'no' or '—' (no online quote/booking path — a concrete gap we can close).\n\nBAD LEAD (cap the final score DOWN — these cannot or will not buy):\n- If reviews is a number under 15 → final score no higher than 2 (too small / not enough activity to have budget).\n- If reviews is a number 500 or more → final score no higher than 4 (likely a multi-location operation with an in-house marketing team — they do not outsource this).\n- If Email is '—' → final score no higher than 3 (we cannot reach them — an unreachable lead is not a lead).\n- If rating is a number below 3.8 → final score no higher than 2 (their problem is service quality, not lead flow).\n- If website is '—' or only a Facebook/Instagram page (no real site) → final score no higher than 3 (too small to pay).\n- If 'Google listing claimed by owner' is 'no' → subtract 2 (unclaimed / neglected listing).\n\nCap the total between 1 and 10.\n\nHARD RULES — do NOT invent data. Score ONLY on the fields above. Do NOT infer or mention: how recent the reviews are, review age, owner replies to reviews, number of locations, employee/company size, franchise or chain status, whether they run ads (the isAdvertisement field can only ever confirm a 'yes' — it can NEVER confirm that a business is not advertising), or any website feature BEYOND the online-estimates field. If a signal is not in the data, ignore it.\n\nReturn an integer 'score' (1 to 10) and a one-line 'reason' that names the specific spending/money and gap signals you used, e.g. 'Running a Maps ad, 5.0 rating on 324 reviews, 50+ photos, custom domain — clearly spending, but offers no online estimates: a quote-capture gap to sell.' Be concrete.",
        "hasOutputParser": true,
        "batching": {}
      },
